Z||000006||Cliff Pennock||09-14-2001||07:03 PM||||Problem is, that our brain is adapted for recognize faces in anything with low detail. That's why we see faces everywhere, in clouds, in smoke, on mars...<P>We see two black spots (eyes), something vertical (nose) and again something almost horizontal (mouth) and our brain immediately makes a face out of it. But honestly, these shapes are far from rare to appear in almost anything. It's like staring at white noise on a tv. The first shape your brain will "see" is a face.<P>Here are two examples. Both pics only have 26x24 pixels, yet we can clearly see the first pic is a face and if we squint our eyes, we can easily recognize who it is. The second pic however, is of something that has much less detail than a face, yet we are unable to recognize it.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/picsamp1.gif"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/picsamp2.gif"> <BR>||||reg||1
